summary:
- topic: New Board Member Introductions
  text: New board members Daniel Feller, George Risch, and Tyler Smith introduced
    themselves, sharing their backgrounds and motivations for joining the board. Student
    representatives also provided updates on school activities.
- topic: Financial Deficit and Spending Freeze
  text: The Finance Committee reported a projected deficit of up to $1.9 million for
    the current fiscal year, primarily due to the newly ratified teachers' contract.
    A non-personnel spending freeze was implemented, with the goal of ending the year
    with a surplus or at least no deficit.
- topic: Extended Learning Opportunities (ELO) Update
  text: Justine Carlisle and student Destiny presented on the ELO program, highlighting
    its success in providing hands-on learning, paid internships, and career exploration
    for students, including a new youth doula initiative.
- topic: Policy Committee Report
  text: The Policy Committee discussed revisions to the School Board Member Ethics
    policy and transferred the temperature control policy to the Buildings & Grounds
    Committee for further development, emphasizing the need for policies that consider
    diverse school facilities.
- topic: Donations and Grants
  text: The board approved over $46,000 in donations and grants from various organizations,
    supporting programs at South Portland Adult Education, Skillin Elementary, Small
    Elementary, South Portland High School, Kaler Elementary, Brown Elementary, South
    Portland Middle School, and Dyer Elementary.
- topic: Community Engagement and Support
  text: Public commenters, including a parent and the SPTA president, expressed concerns
    about the budget deficit's impact and offered community support and resources.
    The superintendent acknowledged the community's willingness to help and the ongoing
    efforts to plan for an education foundation.
- topic: Middle School Athletic Field Status
  text: Board members inquired about the status of the middle school athletic field,
    which remains unusable four years after the new school's construction. The superintendent
    indicated that an update would be provided at the next board meeting following
    recent discussions.
